WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.720
Trump's new space transportation policy could open federal land for heavy lift spaceports directly expanding the sites available for starship and falcon operations. This is SpaceX Daily, episode 77. The order directs federal agencies to identify sites on federal land suitable for heavy and super heavy launch capability. It responds to projected growth in launch demand that exceeds current pad capacity at existing ranges. The policy also calls for streamlined

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:59.100
environmental reviews that could shorten the timeline from site selection to first launch. Reports suggest the changes target both commercial and national security missions. Existing ranges at vandenberg and Cape Canaveral would gain redundancy from any new federal site approved under the directive. The administration states that existing ranges cannot accommodate the expected surge in heavy lift missions. Officials noted the need for at least one additional site rated for super heavy vehicles.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:18.300
SpaceX has already demonstrated rapid reuse on falcon vehicles and is scaling starship flight tests, both of which benefit from additional orbital class pads. Meanwhile, SpaceX posted roles focused on procuring and trading natural gas to meet power generation requirements at its Texas facilities.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:33.120
Starbase and the planned gigabay expansions rely on onsite generation for test stands and production lines. Natural gas supply directly affects the ability to run multiple raptor test cells and starship static fire campaigns in parallel.

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:47.700
The hiring indicates SpaceX is internalizing fuel procurement rather than relying solely on third party suppliers. On site generation capacity must scale with the number of simultaneous engine tests and vehicle stacking operations.

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:57.060
Local officials at Cape Canaveral are evaluating infrastructure, noise, and environmental effects ahead of potential starship operations from the Florida coast.

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:11.220
The study examines road closures, public safety zones, and compatibility with existing falcon and atlas launches. Starships larger propellant load and higher thrust profile create different ground impact footprints than current vehicles.

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:23.020
Findings will inform whether new exclusion zones or mitigation measures are required. The assessment also reviews compatibility with ongoing dragon crew and cargo rotations at the same range.

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:37.400
SpaceX adjusted the test objectives for the upcoming integrated flight test, reducing the number of new hardware demonstrations. The change follows data review from the prior flight and focuses remaining risk on the booster catch hardware.

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:50.560
Ship and booster vehicles remain in flow at starbase, with the next static fire campaign still scheduled before flight. The revised manifest keeps the program on track for an orbital attempt before year end.

00:02:50.560 --> 00:03:12.480
Booster recovery hardware and ship reentry systems continue parallel qualification testing. NASA contracted SpaceX for $843 million to develop the U.S. de-orbit vehicle. The contract covers design and production of a vehicle to safely de-orbit the $945,000 international space station after crew departure.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:25.020
SpaceX will adapt dragon derived systems to control the station's reentry corridor and limit debris footprint. The award adds a new high mass controlled reentry mission to the company's manifest.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:35.580
Work will run in parallel with ongoing crew dragon and cargo rotations. The vehicle must manage the station's mass and attitude during the final de-orbit burn sequence.

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:46.720
Nvidia joins star clouds $250 million orbital data center funding round. The round values the orbital compute startup at $2.3 billion post money.

00:03:47.380 --> 00:04:01.700
Star cloud plans to place GPU clusters in low earth orbit and use laser links for data return. Nvidia's participation signals hardware level interest in space-based inference nodes that could later integrate with starlink constellations.

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:11.920
The funding supports prototype satellite builds and ground segment testing. Laser cross links would enable tasking and results delivery through existing starling ground stations.

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:25.800
Observers on R-slash SpaceX lounge are examining close-up imagery of Starship 40's engine bay, noting differences in corrosion resistance between two stainless steel alloys used on the skirt and thrust structure.

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:36.040
Separate threads analyze manufacturing sequences for the smallest Starship components, including how laser cut and formed parts feed into the main tank assembly line.

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:49.740
Users are also debating the fuel margin required for a control de-orbit burn from a 300 kilometer circular orbit, comparing ISP assumptions for raptor variance against estimated vehicle mass at end of mission.

00:04:50.660 --> 00:05:03.600
Worth keeping an eye on. Bernstein analysts argue that SpaceX mobile faces fundamental physics limits on spectrum reuse and latency that could cap its addressable market even if regulatory approvals are granted.

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:14.060
The report highlights path-loss differences between terrestrial 5G and satellite direct-to-cell links, noting that building density and foliage remain significant barriers.

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:26.060
SpaceX has not yet released measured throughput or coverage maps from the initial direct-to-cell tests. Resolution would require flight data showing consistent service in non-line-of-site conditions.

00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:41.420
On the AI front, Nvidia's participation in Star Cloud's latest round ties GPU supplied directly to orbital node development, with the $250 million raise earmarked for prototype satellites that would use laser cross-links for data movement.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:52.520
The valuation at $2.3 billion reflects expectations that on-orbit inference clusters could later leverage Starlink's existing ground network for taffying and results delivery.

00:05:52.520 --> 00:06:05.380
Separately, GROCBOT has been added to additional X.AI subscription tiers, expanding access to the model family inside the cursor editor environment for developers already using the AI coding tool.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:10.720
The update broadens distribution of GROC capabilities without requiring separate accounts.

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:27.340
The engineering reality behind SpaceX's material choices is that consistent use of 304 L-derived stainless across starship brackets, fittings, and tanks reduces the number of material transitions that must be qualified for cryogenic and reentry environments.

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:37.480
By keeping the alloy family consistent, the company avoids separate heat treatment and passivation protocols that would otherwise multiply inspection steps on the production line.

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:46.780
The raw material cost floor for these parts is dominated by the price of nickel and chromium, plus the energy required to roll and a kneel sheet stock.

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:56.360
Once formed, the finished brackets cost several times that baseline because each still requires precision laser cutting, forming dies, and weld prep machining.

00:06:57.020 --> 00:07:04.880
That idiot index gap shrinks when the same forming tools and weld schedules are reused across thousands of identical brackets per vehicle.

00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:19.700
The approach also simplifies the heat shield attachment interface because tile studs and brackets share the same coefficient of thermal expansion as the tank wall, reducing differential expansion loads during ascent heating and descent plasma exposure.

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:29.040
Watching how quickly these small part processes scale inside the gigabay will indicate whether the overall vehicle production rate can match the targeted flight cadence.

00:07:30.040 --> 00:07:38.340
Consistent alloy selection further supports rapid iteration on bracket geometry without introducing new qualification campaigns for each design tweak.

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:42.520
A quick look at the tape shows SPCX closing higher.

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:52.380
Before we go, the next starship static fire campaign remains the clearest near term marker for whether the revised test objectives still support a year end orbital flight.

